                <h3 style="margin: 1.5rem 0 0.5rem;">Review</h3>
                <p>Matt Haig's "The Midnight Library" is a poignant exploration of regret, choice, and the infinite possibilities of life. The novel follows Nora Seed, a woman who finds herself in a mystical library between life and death, where each book represents a different version of her life had she made different choices.</p>
                
                <p>What makes this novel particularly compelling is its balance between philosophical depth and accessible storytelling. Haig tackles weighty existential questions without ever becoming ponderous. The concept of the midnight library itself is beautifully realized—a place that's both comforting and terrifying, where every book holds both potential salvation and painful revelation.</p>
                
                <p>The novel's greatest strength lies in its emotional honesty. Nora's journey through her possible lives reveals universal truths about human nature—our tendency to romanticize paths not taken, our struggle to appreciate what we have, and our capacity for change. Haig's prose is clean and direct, with moments of genuine lyricism when describing the library and its mysterious caretaker.</p>
                
                <h3 style="margin: 1.5rem 0 0.5rem;">Recommendation</h3>
                <p>Perfect for readers who enjoyed "The Invisible Life of Addie LaRue" or "Life After Life." While the premise might sound melancholic, the book ultimately delivers a hopeful message about the value of ordinary life. It's particularly resonant for anyone who's ever wondered "what if?" about their own choices.</p>
                
                <div style="margin-top: 2rem; padding-top: 1rem; border-top: 1px solid #eee;">
